-- Japan story arc: 3 stories. One framed retrospectively on the
-- now-nearly-3-years-old Qualified Invoice System, one on the
-- genuinely current JP PINT v1.1.3 spec update alongside the upcoming
-- October 2026 relief step-down (today is 5 August 2026), and one on
-- Japan's Peppol Authority role more broadly.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2023-10-01-japan-qualified-invoice-system-effective', '2023-10-01', '2023-10', 'Nearly three years on, Japan''s Qualified Invoice System remains what it always was: a registration and documentation regime tied to consumption-tax input credit -- not an e-invoicing mandate. Paper and PDF invoices are still fully valid.', '<h3>🇯🇵 Japan''s Qualified Invoice System, three years on: still not an e-invoicing mandate</h3>
<p>Japan''s Qualified Invoice System (適格請求書等保存方式) took effect on 1 October 2023, after a two-year registration lead-in that opened in October 2021. It''s easy to assume a country reaching a landmark tax-invoicing date has adopted mandatory e-invoicing -- Japan hasn''t. To claim input tax credit, a business buyer generally needs a "qualified invoice" from a National Tax Agency (NTA)-registered supplier, containing specific content: the issuer''s name and 14-digit T-number, the issuance date, itemized amounts, tax-differentiated totals, and the consumption tax total. Paper and PDF invoices meeting that content bar are fully valid -- nothing requires electronic issuance.</p>
<p>What''s changed since 2023 is the incentive structure around registration, not the format rule. Businesses that buy from suppliers who haven''t registered still get partial input-tax-credit relief on those purchases, but that relief is already tapering down -- from 80% to 70% from October 2026, continuing to 50% in 2028 and 30% in 2030. The pressure to register builds gradually, without a fine attached.</p>
<p>Separately, Japan''s Digital Agency has spent the same period building out JP PINT, a genuinely real but entirely voluntary Peppol-based e-invoicing standard. The two tracks -- mandatory tax documentation and voluntary electronic exchange -- remain deliberately distinct, and conflating them is the single most common misunderstanding about Japan''s compliance landscape.</p>', 'https://www.nta.go.jp/taxes/shiraberu/zeimokubetsu/shohi/keigenzeiritsu/pdf/0024006-039_01.pdf', 1);
